                  through a fixed physics solver.},
}

@inproceedings{jatavallabhula2021gradsim,
  title     = {gradSim: Differentiable Simulation for System
               Identification and Visuomotor Control},
  author    = {Jatavallabhula, Krishna Murthy and Macklin, Miles and
               Golemo, Florian and Voleti, Vikram and Petrini, Linda and
               Weiss, Martin and Considine, Breandan and
               Parent-Levesque, Jerome and Xie, Kevin and Erleben, Kenny
               and Paull, Liam and Shkurti, Florian and Nowrouzezahrai,
               Derek and Fidler, Sanja},
  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations
               (ICLR)},
  year      = {2021},
  eprint    = {2104.02646},
  archivePrefix = {arXiv},
  note      = {Recovers mass, friction, and elasticity from video by
               backpropagating through a differentiable physics engine
               and a differentiable renderer end to end. Our solver
               plays an analogous fixed-decoder role, but is frozen
               rather than optimized, and the target it is trained
               against is a predictor's own next-state belief rather
               than pixels.},
}

@article{mao2024piwm,
  title   = {Physically Interpretable World Models via Weakly
             Supervised Representation Learning},
  author  = {Mao, Zhenjiang and Umasudhan, Mrinall Eashaan and Ruchkin,
             Ivan},
  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2412.12870},
  year    = {2024},
  note    = {Closest prior work in spirit: aligns latent states with
             physical quantities and constrains their evolution with a
             partially-known dynamics model, without ground-truth
             physical annotations. The supervision differs in kind:
             PIWM uses weak, distribution-based physical labels and a
             learnable dynamics model; our training signal uses no
             physical label at all, and the dynamics model is frozen
             rather than learned.},
}

@article{esmati2026invisiblehand,
  title   = {The Invisible Hand of Physics: When Video Diffusion Models
             Know More Than They Show},
  author  = {Esmati, Parsa and Nath, Somjit and Hofmann, Katja and
             Nowrouzezahrai, Derek and Kahou, Samira Ebrahimi and
             Mirmehdi, Majid},
  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.05328},
  year    = {2026},
  note    = {Finds physical plausibility linearly decodable from video
             diffusion transformer states with no self-supervised
             training aimed at physics, evidence that physical
             structure can be present in a representation nobody asked
             for it. Decodability is exactly the reading our own
             \emph{decodable} probe measures; the paper's open question,
             and ours, is whether that decodable signal is also used by
             the model, not just present in it.},
}

@article{li2026ldr,
  title   = {Learning How the World Evolves: Extrapolative Video World
             Models via Latent Dynamics Reasoning},
  author  = {Li, Haodong and Liu, Shaoteng and Wang, Tianyu and Ge,
             Chongjian and Ji, Sihui and Zhang, Jiahan and Lin, Xin and
             Lu, Haolin and Lin, Zhe and Chandraker, Manmohan},
  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2608.09926},
  year    = {2026},
  note    = {Structures a video world model's latent transition as
             explicit kinematic integration: the network learns only a
             higher-order residual while a fixed numerical integrator
             carries the lower-order dynamics, evaluated with a
             physical-scenario-stratified benchmark (uniform motion,
             parabolic trajectories, collision, bouncing, looming) our
             own regime-stratified evaluation follows the shape of. The
             structural difference is where the split happens: LDR
             integrates within a single latent path, while we keep the
             physics computation in a second, independently falsifiable
             head.},
}

@article{zeng2026phylatent,
  title   = {PhyLatent: Learning Dynamics-Relevant Representations for
             JEPA World Models},
  author  = {Zeng, Xi and Ren, Haojie and Song, Ziying},
  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2608.05720},
  year    = {2026},
  note    = {Shows that preventing representational collapse in a JEPA
             world model does not by itself guarantee the latent
             preserves physical state, and targets specific failure
             modes with physical-state grounding and counterfactual
             dynamics objectives. A concurrent, closely related
             diagnosis of the same gap this paper's introduction
             opens with, from the collapse-prevention side rather than
             the self-distillation side.},
}
